WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.280
Why does a small nudge fade, while a slightly bigger one fires a full spike?

2
00:00:08.333 --> 00:00:14.533
Nudge the membrane 5 mV, to minus −60 A few sodium channels open, and a little

3
00:00:14.533 --> 00:00:15.593
sodium flows in.

4
00:00:16.567 --> 00:00:18.407
Potassium current flows out.

5
00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:22.207
Leak is still inward here, below its equilibrium potential.

6
00:00:23.327 --> 00:00:24.867
Together they give more outward current.

7
00:00:25.867 --> 00:00:28.887
Net current is outward, so the voltage drifts back.

8
00:00:29.887 --> 00:00:30.887
Nothing happens.

9
00:00:31.650 --> 00:00:38.190
Now nudge it 12 mV, to minus −53 More sodium channels open, and inward current beats

10
00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.190
outward.

11
00:00:39.633 --> 00:00:43.113
The voltage rises, which opens still more sodium channels.

12
00:00:44.013 --> 00:00:47.573
Inward current grows faster than potassium and leak can oppose it.

13
00:00:48.517 --> 00:00:49.637
That is threshold.

14
00:00:50.817 --> 00:00:54.377
Past it, sodium feedback sustains itself, and the rise runs away.

15
00:00:55.400 --> 00:00:58.080
Inward current at one instant is not enough.

16
00:00:59.000 --> 00:01:02.320
What matters is whether sodium keeps outgrowing the opposing currents.

17
00:01:03.383 --> 00:01:05.723
So threshold is not a fixed voltage.

18
00:01:06.583 --> 00:01:11.403
A slow depolarization inactivates sodium channels and opens potassium channels,

19
00:01:11.903 --> 00:01:12.903
raising it.

20
00:01:14.817 --> 00:01:18.097
Once the rise sustains itself, the spike runs its course.

21
00:01:19.037 --> 00:01:22.597
Its size is set by the channels and the equilibrium potentials.

22
00:01:23.450 --> 00:01:27.030
With brief pushes from rest, a bigger push brings the spike sooner.

23
00:01:27.910 --> 00:01:29.590
Its size stays essentially the same.

24
00:01:30.450 --> 00:01:31.450
That is all-or-none.

25
00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:35.660
Right after a spike, most inactivation gates are shut.

26
00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.540
No stimulus, however strong, fires a second spike.

27
00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:42.480
That is the absolute refractory period.

28
00:01:44.050 --> 00:01:49.450
As those gates reopen, a stronger stimulus can fire a spike, often a smaller one.

29
00:01:50.290 --> 00:01:52.390
That is the relative refractory period.

30
00:01:53.383 --> 00:01:58.203
A stronger push is needed then, because some sodium channels are still inactivated

31
00:01:58.203 --> 00:02:00.883
and potassium conductance is still raised.

32
00:02:01.883 --> 00:02:05.103
At long intervals, the second spike is full size again.

33
00:02:06.067 --> 00:02:07.287
One more question.

34
00:02:08.387 --> 00:02:10.287
Block the potassium channels with TEA.

35
00:02:11.127 --> 00:02:12.507
What happens to the falling phase?

36
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:19.770
From the same starting voltage, the rise is little changed.

37
00:02:20.650 --> 00:02:25.010
The fall is slower, relying on inactivation and leak, so the spike broadens.

38
00:02:25.983 --> 00:02:30.783
The undershoot is shallower too, because it comes from raised potassium conductance.

39
00:02:31.683 --> 00:02:34.803
Threshold is sodium feedback that sustains itself.

40
00:02:35.743 --> 00:02:37.623
Inactivation makes refractoriness.

41
00:02:38.563 --> 00:02:40.403
Blocking potassium broadens the spike.
